WEBVTT

00:00:00.035 --> 00:00:02.821
Coding standards are agreements about how software should

00:00:02.844 --> 00:00:03.994
be written and maintained.

00:00:04.435 --> 00:00:07.512
You do not have to read every line of code to benefit from

00:00:07.558 --> 00:00:07.837
them.

00:00:08.278 --> 00:00:11.865
Ask whether a module has a clear responsibility, whether

00:00:11.958 --> 00:00:15.186
names explain intent, whether inputs are checked at

00:00:15.244 --> 00:00:17.891
boundaries, and whether the next person can find the

00:00:17.914 --> 00:00:18.460
behavior.

00:00:19.157 --> 00:00:21.734
Standards should help the agent create software that can

00:00:21.780 --> 00:00:24.787
be understood and changed without relying on the original

00:00:24.857 --> 00:00:25.658
author's memory.

00:00:25.968 --> 00:00:29.219
A silent failure happens when something goes wrong but the

00:00:29.312 --> 00:00:30.786
system hides the problem.

00:00:31.483 --> 00:00:35.302
Imagine the inquiry tracker says "Thank you" even though

00:00:35.325 --> 00:00:36.614
the record was not saved.

00:00:37.055 --> 00:00:40.318
That is worse than a visible failure because both visitor

00:00:40.399 --> 00:00:42.791
and owner believe the inquiry exists.

00:00:43.395 --> 00:00:46.460
A helpful diagnostic states what failed, where it

00:00:46.518 --> 00:00:49.908
happened, and what action may help, without exposing

00:00:49.978 --> 00:00:53.786
passwords, private customer content, or secret keys.

00:00:54.296 --> 00:00:57.187
The user-facing message and operator log can contain

00:00:57.280 --> 00:00:58.639
different levels of detail.

00:00:58.902 --> 00:01:01.270
A test is a repeatable check of a claim.

00:01:01.711 --> 00:01:04.579
Boundary tests exercise the points where data enters,

00:01:04.788 --> 00:01:06.123
leaves, or changes state.

00:01:06.564 --> 00:01:10.454
For the tracker, try valid and invalid submissions, an

00:01:10.581 --> 00:01:13.252
unauthorized attempt to view records, and a storage

00:01:13.298 --> 00:01:13.786
failure.

00:01:14.390 --> 00:01:16.479
Use synthetic data for examples.

00:01:16.921 --> 00:01:20.961
Test both the result and important non-effects: an invalid

00:01:21.007 --> 00:01:24.699
submission should not create a record, and a failed save

00:01:24.757 --> 00:01:26.534
should not show false success.

00:01:27.137 --> 00:01:30.342
Ask the agent to explain which requirement each test

00:01:30.377 --> 00:01:31.062
supports.

00:01:31.320 --> 00:01:35.023
A coherent slice includes the change, its relevant check,

00:01:35.407 --> 00:01:38.425
evidence, and an update to the project record.

00:01:39.122 --> 00:01:42.105
A commit is a saved revision in version control.

00:01:42.802 --> 00:01:45.530
Small commits can make a problem easier to locate and

00:01:45.588 --> 00:01:46.181
reverse.

00:01:46.622 --> 00:01:49.327
They are not automatically proof that behavior works.

00:01:49.768 --> 00:01:53.263
Keep scope and ownership clear, preserve unrelated work,

00:01:53.321 --> 00:01:54.888
and run the affected checks.

00:01:55.492 --> 00:01:58.661
Repeat checks when the underlying code changes, a check

00:01:58.743 --> 00:02:01.146
fails, or a new concern justifies it.

00:02:01.520 --> 00:02:03.714
Implemented means present in the code.

00:02:04.155 --> 00:02:07.383
Offline-tested means tested in a controlled environment.

00:02:07.766 --> 00:02:10.552
Provider-accepted means an outside service accepted a

00:02:10.622 --> 00:02:11.272
request.

00:02:11.969 --> 00:02:14.848
Live-observed means the intended real-world effect was

00:02:14.941 --> 00:02:16.056
actually observed.

00:02:16.752 --> 00:02:18.180
These are different claims.

00:02:18.563 --> 00:02:22.174
For example, a fake email provider returning success

00:02:22.244 --> 00:02:25.680
proves the application handled the fake response; it does

00:02:25.762 --> 00:02:26.899
not prove delivery.

00:02:27.224 --> 00:02:30.429
A provider accepting a real message does not necessarily

00:02:30.498 --> 00:02:32.228
prove the intended person received it.

00:02:32.527 --> 00:02:35.627
An evidence record identifies the version, commands or

00:02:35.685 --> 00:02:40.155
observations, results, time, and sanitized artifacts.

00:02:40.665 --> 00:02:44.102
Missing access is a blocker or an unverified gap, not a

00:02:44.148 --> 00:02:44.648
pass.

00:02:45.251 --> 00:02:48.676
Ask what happens when a change fails, how to restore the

00:02:48.734 --> 00:02:51.938
earlier state, and whether the restoration was actually

00:02:52.008 --> 00:02:52.577
rehearsed.

00:02:53.273 --> 00:02:56.663
Good review is proportionate to risk: protect critical

00:02:56.733 --> 00:02:59.345
boundaries while avoiding repeated full reviews that add

00:02:59.403 --> 00:03:01.006
cost without new information.

00:03:01.327 --> 00:03:04.287
Imagine that six months from now the tracker fails and a

00:03:04.357 --> 00:03:05.820
new agent must diagnose it.

00:03:06.331 --> 00:03:10.197
Names like saveInquiry and listNewInquiries communicate

00:03:10.232 --> 00:03:11.973
purpose more clearly than doStuff.

00:03:12.577 --> 00:03:15.978
A module with a narrow responsibility is easier to locate

00:03:16.025 --> 00:03:19.809
and change than one that handles validation, storage,

00:03:20.193 --> 00:03:22.665
email, and presentation in a single block.

00:03:23.362 --> 00:03:26.508
Ask the agent for a map of dependencies and side effects.

00:03:26.891 --> 00:03:29.933
You do not need to prescribe every formatting preference

00:03:30.107 --> 00:03:33.022
to require that future readers can follow the system.

00:03:33.327 --> 00:03:37.088
If a required field is missing, a visitor can see "Enter a

00:03:37.147 --> 00:03:40.258
way for us to contact you." If the storage service is

00:03:40.328 --> 00:03:43.636
down, the owner may need an operational log naming the

00:03:43.683 --> 00:03:46.469
failing component and a safe troubleshooting action.

00:03:47.166 --> 00:03:50.324
Logs should avoid recording sensitive contact details,

00:03:50.475 --> 00:03:54.132
credentials, or raw external payloads unnecessarily.

00:03:54.573 --> 00:03:57.696
The point is not to make every error public; it is to

00:03:57.789 --> 00:04:00.680
ensure the right person can understand and respond to the

00:04:00.726 --> 00:04:01.191
failure.

00:04:01.538 --> 00:04:03.907
A negative test verifies behavior when something should

00:04:03.942 --> 00:04:04.580
not happen.

00:04:05.021 --> 00:04:08.365
For example, submit an inquiry without a contact method.

00:04:08.806 --> 00:04:11.302
Confirm the system rejects it and does not create a

00:04:11.360 --> 00:04:11.767
record.

00:04:12.208 --> 00:04:15.215
Attempt to open the owner view without authorization and

00:04:15.273 --> 00:04:16.817
verify access is denied.

00:04:17.258 --> 00:04:20.799
Simulate a storage failure and verify the public form does

00:04:20.916 --> 00:04:22.262
not display success.

00:04:22.704 --> 00:04:25.560
These checks often catch mistakes that a single successful

00:04:25.618 --> 00:04:26.535
example misses.

00:04:26.871 --> 00:04:29.786
A local test may replace a provider with a fake response.

00:04:30.227 --> 00:04:32.862
That can prove your application reacts correctly to that

00:04:32.932 --> 00:04:36.566
response, but it cannot prove the real provider accepts

00:04:36.601 --> 00:04:38.470
your account or delivers a message.

00:04:39.166 --> 00:04:42.336
A provider's acceptance is a stronger but still different

00:04:42.382 --> 00:04:44.832
observation from a real user seeing the effect.

00:04:45.436 --> 00:04:47.711
Record the environment and the exact claim.

00:04:48.094 --> 00:04:51.171
"Not yet tested" is a useful status when it keeps the next

00:04:51.217 --> 00:04:53.017
person from assuming proof exists.

00:04:53.327 --> 00:04:56.345
A reviewer can ask: which requirement does this change

00:04:56.380 --> 00:04:56.833
implement?

00:04:57.216 --> 00:04:58.644
What inputs does it accept?

00:04:59.027 --> 00:05:00.374
What happens on failure?

00:05:00.699 --> 00:05:02.069
Which tests changed?

00:05:02.452 --> 00:05:04.879
Are secrets or private data exposed?

00:05:05.320 --> 00:05:06.969
Is there an obvious rollback?

00:05:07.352 --> 00:05:09.941
Does the documentation still agree with the behavior?

00:05:10.544 --> 00:05:13.575
A small verified commit keeps these questions manageable.

00:05:14.016 --> 00:05:17.278
Avoid rerunning the same complete suite repeatedly when

00:05:17.336 --> 00:05:20.459
nothing relevant has changed, but do run checks again when

00:05:20.494 --> 00:05:23.374
a change or failure makes earlier results stale.

00:05:23.681 --> 00:05:26.792
An evidence note can say: at this revision, the local

00:05:26.839 --> 00:05:30.380
validation suite ran and its stated cases passed; a

00:05:30.496 --> 00:05:32.864
separate live-provider check has not been run.

00:05:33.247 --> 00:05:36.034
Include the command and sanitized output when appropriate.

00:05:36.417 --> 00:05:39.157
If a screenshot shows a private list, use fictional

00:05:39.215 --> 00:05:39.703
records.

00:05:40.086 --> 00:05:42.768
The result should let another person verify the level of

00:05:42.837 --> 00:05:46.286
confidence without needing the original agent's memory or

00:05:46.344 --> 00:05:48.282
a polished summary that hides gaps.

00:05:48.563 --> 00:05:51.489
Ask the agent to pair each important standard with a

00:05:51.570 --> 00:05:52.917
concrete observation.

00:05:53.300 --> 00:05:56.470
For example, rather than saying "errors are handled," show

00:05:56.528 --> 00:05:59.744
the test or code path that demonstrates a failed save does

00:05:59.778 --> 00:06:01.323
not produce a success message.

00:06:01.926 --> 00:06:05.258
Rather than saying "security is complete," describe which

00:06:05.351 --> 00:06:08.509
access boundary was checked, in which environment, and

00:06:08.556 --> 00:06:10.135
what remains outside the test.

00:06:10.576 --> 00:06:13.606
Where the reviewer cannot reproduce or inspect a claim,

00:06:13.850 --> 00:06:15.707
mark the confidence accordingly.

00:06:16.404 --> 00:06:19.307
This practice applies even if you do not personally read

00:06:19.330 --> 00:06:20.317
the implementation.
